WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:03.300
Buried here, deep in a Japanese mountain pass,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:10.380
is one of the world's rarest technologies. A&nbsp;
43-kilometer-long magnetic levitation train track.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.180
Which will eventually become part of the&nbsp;
world's first inter-city Maglev train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.640
connecting Tokyo and Osaka.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:23.160
With the power of superconducting magnets,&nbsp;
these trains hover a mere 10 centimeters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:29.160
above their track, while pummeling along at a&nbsp;
mind-boggling speed of 500 kilometers per hour.

00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:33.780
With no physical contact required with&nbsp;
the track, they can operate in any weather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:39.540
conditions and have lower maintenance costs&nbsp;
due to reduced friction and fewer moving parts.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:44.640
Despite the advantages of Maglev trains&nbsp;
over traditional high-speed trains,

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.260
The only commercial high-speed Maglev&nbsp;
line currently in operation is just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.160
30 kilometers long. Connecting&nbsp;
Shanghai Airport to it’s city.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:59.940
A journey the train makes in only 8 minutes,&nbsp;
but a track this short largely defeats the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:05.160
advantage of high speed maglev, by the time&nbsp;
the train gets up to speed, it needs to slow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:10.080
down again. A 30 kilometer journey in 8&nbsp;
minutes actually equates to an average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.620
speed of 225 kilometers per hour, slower than&nbsp;
the top speed of conventional high speed rail.

00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:22.440
The planned future network of high-speed&nbsp;
maglev tracks, just 400 kilometers long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:28.920
and it pales in comparison to the extensive&nbsp;
network of 60,000km of regular high-speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:34.920
train tracks already in place around the world,&nbsp;
with an additional 50,000km under construction.

00:01:34.920 --> 00:01:40.320
Why is this gap so large? Let's&nbsp;
delve deeper into how Maglev&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.040
trains work and explore the reasons&nbsp;
behind their restricted deployment.

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:56.280
We have two primary methods of levitating&nbsp;
a train. We can pull the train up using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.080
attractive forces or push the train&nbsp;
upward using repulsive forces.  
 &nbsp;

00:02:01.080 --> 00:02:06.840
The pulling approach involves a combination&nbsp;
of a magnetic iron rail and an electromagnet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.760
that uses attractive forces to raise&nbsp;
the body of the train. This is an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.760 --> 00:02:15.720
active system where the gap between&nbsp;
the rail and the magnet is crucial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.400
as the strength of the attractive force&nbsp;
decreases with the square of the distance.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.840
Meaning, the train needs active&nbsp;
controls and feedback loops ensure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:29.940
that these gaps stay between 8 and&nbsp;
12 mm. If the train begins to drop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:36.060
the magnetic force holding it up will quickly&nbsp;
weaken, causing it to fall even further. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:41.460
an unstable configuration. These types of&nbsp;
trains are referred to as Electromagnetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.920
Suspension trains. This is the system the 30&nbsp;
kilometer line in Shanghai uses. [1][REF_00]

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:50.700
Japan opted to use a different&nbsp;
system, with a more significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.820
gap of 10 centimeters between the track and train.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:59.700
8 times greater than the gap for electromagnetic&nbsp;
suspension, while also providing a passively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:04.980
stable method of levitation. A vital property&nbsp;
to have in the earthquake prone region.[2]

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:11.220
In this approach, magnets onboard the train&nbsp;
interact with passive coils on the track. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.220 --> 00:03:15.240
the train moves over these coils, they&nbsp;
experience a changing magnetic field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:19.080
which generates the opposing&nbsp;
field that lifts the train upward.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:23.940
If the train is stationary on top of&nbsp;
the coils, there is no change in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:29.700
magnetic field. So this dynamic system only&nbsp;
works when the train is already going fast.

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:34.080
The trains have wheels that drop&nbsp;
down when the train is slowing down,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:38.640
but flip into the carriage&nbsp;
when they reach 100 km/h. [1]

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:43.740
In order to achieve a stable position on&nbsp;
the track, the north and south poles are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.600
arranged vertically on the sides of the track.&nbsp;
The magnets are formed from coils of wire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:52.980
arranged in a figure 8 pattern. A&nbsp;
south pole points upwards on one side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.140
while the north pole points up on the other.

00:03:55.140 --> 00:03:59.880
If the train were to drop slightly or&nbsp;
move sideways, the magnets on the train&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:04.680
induce currents on the opposite coils,&nbsp;
generating a stronger magnetic field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:09.150
which pushes the train back to the&nbsp;
midpoint of the figure 8 shapes. [3]

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:13.800
This dynamic system is forgiving, with&nbsp;
the 10 centimeter gaps between the track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.800 --> 00:04:18.540
and the train naturally maintained with&nbsp;
no need for complex control inputs.[1]

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:25.560
However, the Japanese Maglev train, called L0,&nbsp;
will be the first of its kind. These trains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:31.200
known as SCmaglevs, need superconducting&nbsp;
coils to generate the extremely powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:36.060
permanent magnetic field on the train.&nbsp;
These are the coils that induce the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:40.620
changing magnetic field and allow the train&nbsp;
to levitate in a stable configuration.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:45.960
These coils, located on each side of the&nbsp;
carriage, must be maintained below their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:52.680
critical temperature to ensure their current flows&nbsp;
without resistance. The niobium-titanium coils are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:58.140
cooled using liquid helium and placed inside&nbsp;
a container that is cooled by liquid nitrogen.

00:04:58.140 --> 00:05:01.140
The coils are cooled using a Pulse Tube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:04.260
Refrigerator [4]. Which uses&nbsp;
sound waves to cool the helium.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:10.500
The same type of refrigeration cycle that the&nbsp;
James Webb telescope is using to keep its infrared&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:16.080
sensors cold enough to detect even the faintest&nbsp;
infrared heat radiation from the distant past.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:21.300
A sound wave is just a pressure wave and&nbsp;
pressure and temperature are directly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.480
proportional. Higher pressure&nbsp;
will cause higher temperature.

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:30.300
We can take advantage of this by creating a&nbsp;
standing wave, where the peaks and troughs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:35.820
of the wave are stationary. We can do this in a&nbsp;
closed tube where the resonant frequency of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:41.400
tube is determined by the tube's length. Here&nbsp;
the sound wave will bounce off the closed end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:46.860
and create a region of compression and high&nbsp;
pressure, and therefore high temperature.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:52.680
We can now place a stack that spans this&nbsp;
hot region at the end of the tube to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.680 --> 00:05:58.320
cold region at the centre. Now if we place&nbsp;
a heat exchangeron either end of the stack,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:02.640
we can now transfer the heat to a&nbsp;
radiator and the cold side will conduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:06.300
heat away from the superconducting&nbsp;
coils. Keeping them cold. [REF] [5]

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:10.680
There are eight of these coils&nbsp;
per car, with four on each side.

00:06:10.680 --> 00:06:16.440
However, having a magnetic field strength&nbsp;
of this magnitude right next to passengers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:22.260
and traveling by at half the speed of sound,&nbsp;
could cause some serious problems. Ferrous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:27.720
objects aren’t allowed anywhere near MRI&nbsp;
machines. The engineers needed to find a way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.720 --> 00:06:34.260
to ensure the magnetic field did not extend into&nbsp;
the passenger cabins, or far away from the train.&nbsp;

00:06:34.260 --> 00:06:38.640
We can’t block magnetic fields,&nbsp;
but we can redirect them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:43.920
in the same way we can conduct electricity&nbsp;
to where we want it to go using copper wires.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:47.940
Where we measure the ease of passage&nbsp;
for an electric current with resistance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:52.560
we measure the ease of passage for a&nbsp;
magnetic field with reluctance. Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.160
copper has a low resistance,&nbsp;
Iron has a low reluctance.

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:02.760
We can use electric steel shielding to divert the&nbsp;
magnetic field away from the cabin and stations.

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:08.760
Electric steel contains 3% silicon and is&nbsp;
post-processed to produce large crystal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.760 --> 00:07:15.540
grains aligned in a specific direction, which is&nbsp;
controlled by a cold rolling process. The grain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:20.880
oriented electrical steel can carry about&nbsp;
30% more magnetic flux in this direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:26.250
allowing engineers to direct the magnetic&nbsp;
flux in the desired direction. [6]

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:32.340
However, this shield is heavy, so we want&nbsp;
to use as little of it as possible. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:37.320
we can also tailor the magnetic field shape&nbsp;
through careful design of the magnets themselves.

00:07:37.320 --> 00:07:42.600
If we placed two parallel magnets like&nbsp;
this, north poles aligned horizontally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.600 --> 00:07:47.940
the magnetic flux lines would run parallel&nbsp;
to the magnets and be densers between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.940 --> 00:07:53.160
magnets. However, if we flip one of them,&nbsp;
the magnetic flux lines connect up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:58.020
create a kind of bubble in the between them. The&nbsp;
magnetic field will be weaker in this location.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:03.840
This is how we arrange the coils on either&nbsp;
side of the cabin. There are 4 sets of coils&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:09.000
on each side. So we also need to flip the&nbsp;
magnets so the north and south poles face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:13.650
each other. This creates a low magnetic&nbsp;
field bubble through the corridors. [7]

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:18.060
With the use of shielding, the field&nbsp;
strength is reduced to only 0.5mT,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.350
almost equivalent to Earth's&nbsp;
the magnetic field. [7]

00:08:22.350 --> 00:08:27.300
Of course, near the magnets the field's&nbsp;
strength is unavoidably high, and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:31.800
prevents us from using certain materials&nbsp;
in these areas. The tracks for example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.800 --> 00:08:37.260
need to be constructed from low-magnetic&nbsp;
steel, or fibre reinforced composites. [8]

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:41.400
But how does a levitating train propel&nbsp;
itself if it’s not touching the ground?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.400 --> 00:08:47.160
For conventional rail we just apply a torque to&nbsp;
the wheels that will push off against the ground.

00:08:47.160 --> 00:08:50.700
Wheels are actually a bit of a middleman in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.700 --> 00:08:55.560
development of torque. Many trains use&nbsp;
electric motors to turn their wheels.

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.680
The electric motors use electromagnetic force&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:03.060
to turn a shaft. What if we could&nbsp;
take the same motor and unwrap it.

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:07.260
This is called a linear motor.&nbsp;
Where alternating coils attract&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.260 --> 00:09:09.660
and repel the train with precise timing. [2]

00:09:09.660 --> 00:09:15.840
To brake the trains can use this system in&nbsp;
reverse, this is regenerative braking. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:21.480
electrodynamic systems don’t work at low speed,&nbsp;
they also have brakes for their wheels. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:26.820
trains also feature an air brake that can slow&nbsp;
the train down very effectively at top speeds.

00:09:26.820 --> 00:09:30.780
There is one other challenge when&nbsp;
dealing with contactless trains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:37.140
how do they get electricity? For lower-speed&nbsp;
Maglev systems they use a low friction third rail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:41.190
common in metro systems, to bring&nbsp;
electricity to the trains. [REF][2]

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:44.700
But as speeds get higher this isn't&nbsp;
practical [REF][REF_00] as it defeats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:48.960
the purpose of reducing friction from&nbsp;
the rails. Some early versions of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.940
Japanese style electrodynamic system&nbsp;
actually had a gas turbine onboard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.940 --> 00:09:59.220
provide power. But it now uses a linear&nbsp;
induction coil to collect power from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:03.600
changing magnetic field from the guiding&nbsp;
coils.[REF][REF] [9]. This does create a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:08.220
magnetic drag force, but it’s more effective&nbsp;
than carrying a large generator on board.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:14.400
The Japanese Line is set to be the first&nbsp;
long-distance high-speed Maglev train. Its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:20.280
first phase, covering 285 km, will connect&nbsp;
Tokyo to Nagoya and is scheduled to open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.280 --> 00:10:27.120
by 2027. The second phase, which will&nbsp;
complete the remaining 438 km to Osaka,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:29.940
is expected to be completed a decade later.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:36.960
So why is Japan pursuing this futuristic&nbsp;
technology? Japan has long been at the forefront&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:43.860
of train technology. After opening their Tokyo to&nbsp;
Osaka Shinkansen line in 1964, the world's first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.860 --> 00:10:50.700
high speed train, they immediately began exploring&nbsp;
ways to reduce the 4 journey to under 1 hour.

00:10:50.700 --> 00:10:57.420
Starting in the 1970s, Japan Airways and the&nbsp;
Japanese Central Rail Station constructed a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.420 --> 00:11:03.780
7km testing track here in.Miyazaki They began with&nbsp;
prototypes of trains that could reach speeds of 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:13.200
km/h in 1972, and by 1995, they had developed&nbsp;
trains capable of reaching up to 411 km/h.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.240
As the Miyazaki test track became too short,&nbsp;
the Yamanashi test track began construction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:25.920
in 1990. This 20km test track was conveniently&nbsp;
located between the cities of Tokyo and Nagoya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:32.280
along the route of the upcoming new Maglev line.&nbsp;
Here, they transformed the design from a prototype&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:40.860
to a fully functional train capable of carrying&nbsp;
700 passengers in 12 cars at speeds of 500 km/h.

00:11:40.860 --> 00:11:46.380
But, high-speed Maglev trains are more&nbsp;
than just a science experiment. They are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.380 --> 00:11:50.400
an efficient and super fast way to move&nbsp;
people. But, building these trains is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:55.200
more than just an engineering challenge,&nbsp;
they are also an economic challenge where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:11:59.640
they have to compete against cars,&nbsp;
planes, and other types of trains.

00:11:59.640 --> 00:12:05.400
Whenever Maglevs are brought up in the US, the&nbsp;
idea of traveling between New York and LA in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.400 --> 00:12:11.520
7 hours is often used as a statement of its&nbsp;
potential. It’s the very first line on the US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.520 --> 00:12:17.670
Department of Energy's own website. But a track&nbsp;
of this length makes little economic sense. [10]

00:12:17.670 --> 00:12:22.560
If we plot the travel time for trains,&nbsp;
planes, and cars between say San Francisco&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:27.540
and LA. Two large population centers&nbsp;
within a reasonable driving distance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:31.800
this is what it looks like. Trains&nbsp;
are outright the best option.

00:12:31.800 --> 00:12:36.720
Even though planes are faster, airports&nbsp;
are usually located far outside the city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:41.580
and security wait times increase the length&nbsp;
of the journey. A high speed train will save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:47.460
passengers time, and in turn make an economy&nbsp;
more productive. A reduction of travel time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:55.080
of 2 hours for 900 passengers equates&nbsp;
to 1800 hours. Equivalent to 75 days.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.080 --> 00:13:00.300
Faster trains simply extend the range at&nbsp;
which trains are competitive to airlines.

00:13:00.300 --> 00:13:04.800
Large cities between 200 and 800 kilometers apart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.800 --> 00:13:09.840
are perfect contenders for high speed&nbsp;
rail. DC to New York in just 60 minutes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.440 --> 00:13:16.770
This map shows the potential economically viable&nbsp;
high speed rail connections in the US. [11]

00:13:16.770 --> 00:13:21.840
Unfortunately, as it stands, the US is&nbsp;
not investing much in high speed rail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:27.600
and Maglev systems are even harder to build.&nbsp;
They require completely new tracks that require&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:33.780
electromagnetic coils along the entire length&nbsp;
and specialized materials that are not affected&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.780 --> 00:13:40.200
by the incredibly strong magnetic fields passing&nbsp;
by them at 500 kilometers per hour. The Japanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:47.460
line that connects Tokyo to Nagoya recently&nbsp;
had a price increase to 13.7 billion dollars.

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:54.420
Maglevs are between 10 and 50 times more expensive&nbsp;
than high speed rail. The incredibly successful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.420 --> 00:14:00.600
French high speed rail, the TGV built in&nbsp;
the 80s and 90s cost 2 million dollars per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.600 --> 00:14:07.530
kilometer [12]. The first stage of the Japanese&nbsp;
line is set to cost 77 million dollars per km [1]

00:14:07.530 --> 00:14:12.480
Even when compared to the same route&nbsp;
from Tokyo to Osaka via High-speed rail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:18.180
the maglev system will cost 11.3 times&nbsp;
more per kilometer. That is a large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.180 --> 00:14:21.420
price to pay to reduce the travel time by 1 hour.

00:14:21.420 --> 00:14:27.180
The Tokyo-Nagoya line is taking a more&nbsp;
direct route through mountainous terrain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.180 --> 00:14:31.380
a staggering 86% of the track&nbsp;
would be underground. [13]

00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:37.980
Tunneling over 200 km significantly increases&nbsp;
costs. Interestingly, this also leads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:44.760
increased operational expenses. When the train&nbsp;
travels in the open air at speeds of 500 km/h,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:44.760 --> 00:14:51.360
the unobstructed air simply moves aside. However,&nbsp;
when traveling through a tunnel, the air has no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:57.720
escape route, this creates additional drag which&nbsp;
in turn increases the force required to propel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.720 --> 00:14:58.980
the train forward.
[14]

00:14:58.980 --> 00:15:03.960
The energy usage of the new line will&nbsp;
30% higher compared to the existing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:09.060
Tokyo-Osaka line . [14] But that’s&nbsp;
still vastly better than flying .[15]

00:15:09.060 --> 00:15:14.340
For now, I don't see Maglev technology being&nbsp;
used beyond some special projects like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.340 --> 00:15:21.113
The radical increase in costs simply do not&nbsp;
justify the increases in top speed over high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:21.113 --> 00:15:26.580
speed rail. And it’s beyond imagination for&nbsp;
the US, we are struggling to get regular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.580 --> 00:15:31.440
rail service even in places uniquely&nbsp;
suited to it like the Texas triangle.

00:15:31.440 --> 00:15:35.160
4 high population cities located in a triangle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.160 --> 00:15:39.120
with only the hellscape of&nbsp;
interstate highways connecting them.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:42.600
With the recent speculation about&nbsp;
the potential of room temperature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:47.340
superconductors, a technology that would&nbsp;
make maglev trains vastly easier to build,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:51.660
interest online in superconductor&nbsp;
technologies is higher than ever.

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:56.880
But a lot of it is unintuitive, and&nbsp;
actually understanding how magnetic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.880 --> 00:16:02.040
fields work is a fantastic tool in an&nbsp;
engineer's arsenal. So many of our most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.040 --> 00:16:07.260
important technologies depend on this physical&nbsp;
phenomenon. But, you don't have to pay for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.260 --> 00:16:12.600
whole university level course to understand&nbsp;
the fundamentals. There's a free way you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.600 --> 00:16:17.220
learn about magnetism and electricity,&nbsp;
with this fantastic course on Brilliant.

00:16:17.220 --> 00:16:22.320
